#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338861 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73ad04f5-18e2-5aca-9d12-416ed6d4d2ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-70970" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $M(-14,1,2)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $M$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338862 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2e1c378-a183-516b-92fd-1753dcbc375f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70972" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t,\\hspace{0.167em}s\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨0,-1,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨1,2,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-5,3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-5,3,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338863 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cd142bb-0e37-5169-9871-c05d6282caa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-70974" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t,\\hspace{0.167em}s\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨3,0,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨1,-1,1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨4,1,-3⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338864 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1d4f201-ef5e-5eaf-9add-578e8923ebb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-70976" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(1,\\sqrt{2},-1)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,11,\\frac{1}{3}⟩+s⟨1,2,-3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,\\sqrt{2},5⟩+t⟨-1,0,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨1,2,-3⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨-1,0,3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $C(1,\\sqrt{2},-1)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{c}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩+s⟨1,2,-3⟩+t⟨-1,0,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338865 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63af645f-746e-5e92-b7a0-0217edd376c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-70977" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $D(6,2,1)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,9⟩+s⟨4,-1,5⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨\\sqrt{3},10,-1⟩+t⟨0,2,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨4,-1,5⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨0,2,3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $D(6,2,1)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{d}=⟨6,2,1⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{d}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨6,2,1⟩+s⟨4,-1,5⟩+t⟨0,2,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338866 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc807ce5-aee1-5714-82d8-8391abd01453" :question/math-academy-id "q-70978" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $E(16,-1,3)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=⟨9,-7,2⟩+s⟨5,1,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨15,2,-3⟩+t⟨1,11,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨5,1,-2⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨1,11,3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $E(16,-1,3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{e}=⟨16,-1,3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{e}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨16,-1,3⟩+s⟨5,1,-2⟩+t⟨1,11,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338879 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d249e59-e724-5332-83de-2d46399e2dbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-71034" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $L(-1,-1,1),\\hspace{0.167em}M(2,5,-3)$, and $N(0,-8,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =⟨2,5,-3⟩-⟨-1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =⟨0,-8,-1⟩-⟨-1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-7,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{l}=⟨-1,-1,1⟩$ of the point $l,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{l}+s\\overset{\\to }{LM}+t\\overset{\\to }{LN} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨-1,-1,1⟩+s⟨3,6,-4⟩+t⟨1,-7,-2⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338881 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "238f448c-c904-543b-b4aa-7c48d6e1e778" :question/math-academy-id "q-71038" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $U(2,-1,0),\\hspace{0.167em}V(3,4,-3)$, and $W(2,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{UW},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨3,4,-3⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,5,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{UW} & =⟨2,0,0⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,-1,0⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{u}+s\\overset{\\to }{UV}+t\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨2,-1,0⟩+s⟨1,5,-3⟩+t⟨0,1,0⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341848 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e679b14-a7d9-5eaa-ba4a-ea8d4a8b2fc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72716" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(X)=-13.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the second and the fifth rows). What is the value of $det(Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(-13) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341849 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b0644d5-53aa-5532-b39e-bdadeb30e9f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72723" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $det(B)=91.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{6}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the second and the sixth rows). What is the value of $det(C)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-det(B) \\\\  & =-(91) \\\\  & =-91.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341850 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef160bb4-443b-58b5-915b-9dfda073ef96" :question/math-academy-id "q-72729" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3y \\\\ 2x &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=0 \\\\ 2x &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341851 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2858ce5b-8e2a-5132-aac3-acedb511e7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72731" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=-64.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{8}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{5}{8}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\frac{5}{8}det(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot (-64) \\\\  & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341852 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae55f4eb-c009-586b-a5eb-f77f0f124cd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72733" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=y+1 \\\\ x-y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y &=1 \\\\ x-y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341853 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc0bf99b-722a-5c69-a1c9-0059727d8fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72738" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x &=3y \\\\ y &=-x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x-3y &=0 \\\\ x+y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341854 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e092bdb9-fca3-5ab8-88ef-c95e1a9b5b5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72739" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(B)=11.$ What is the value of $det(2B)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot B)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2B) & ={2}^{5}\\cdot det(B) \\\\  & =32\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =352.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341855 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bce6072f-0fd1-50b6-a305-a94b72b85f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72740" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(Y)=-3.$ What is the value of $det(4Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot Y)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=4$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(4Y) & ={4}^{3}\\cdot det(Y) \\\\  & =64\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-192.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341856 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31c24ae4-5a26-5d23-82f7-e8e80b937d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72752" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=-10.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{4}↔{R}_{6}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{4}↔{R}_{6}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{5},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{5}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $\\frac{1}{5}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}det(B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342000 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99972d7b-41c2-573c-8100-907314a5a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-73524" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & -16 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 12 & -6\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & -16 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 12 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 12 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342006 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a069e33-ab79-5e52-8347-ee8dc3251c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-73535" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & k \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=6+3k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=6+3k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2-(-1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6+3k}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2+k} \\\\ 2+k & =4 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342008 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33a7f588-04f8-5fc0-9705-3e73eb1453a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73542" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $1.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2-0)+(-2k-0)+0 \\\\  & =-2k-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1-(-3)) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k-2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k+1} & =1 \\\\ 2 & =k+1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342009 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93b0be09-4ca6-5085-9855-e90445a7accc" :question/math-academy-id "q-73545" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{4}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-k-6)-(0-3) \\\\  & =-2k-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{11}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{11} & =(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k-9}\\cdot (-k-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+6}{2k+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{11}^{′}=\\frac{4}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+6}{2k+9} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 5(k+6) & =4(2k+9) \\\\ 5k+30 & =8k+36 \\\\ 3k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342014 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6905816-c0d6-59f0-9ff1-518732f97672" :question/math-academy-id "q-73562" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not invertible” is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solution for some $2\\times 1$ column vectors $\\mathbf{b}.$” is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342015 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12c8cd48-ec61-5529-9409-02950b9c8c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73563" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)=0$ is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The system $Ax=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution” is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements “The system $Ax=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution”  and “$A$ is invertible” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342016 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b4e1216-e41e-5317-bf20-2acb3db54b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-73565" :question/problem "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)=0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement “The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point” is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist” is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements $\\det(A)=0$ and “${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342017 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "946ddb30-e9a3-52e2-ad46-5aa2c1f15eef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73567" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)=0$ is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points” is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement “$A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$” is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement “$A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342018 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb595552-3588-5923-aa25-072e92fab82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73570" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k-8\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 8.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has infinitely many solutions” is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect” is false.  According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement $\\det(A)\\ne 0$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342020 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fabb78b-3a40-50c6-9149-8d79603a31f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73579" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+6y-3z &=18 \\\\ -4x+8y-3z &=2 \\\\ y-4z &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-3 & 6 & -3 & 18 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-3 & 6 & -3 & 18 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -22\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z &=-6 \\\\ y-4z &=-3 \\\\ z &=-22,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=-22$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4(-22) &=-3\\,⟹\\,y=-91.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-91$ and $z=-22$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2(-91)+(-22) &=-6\\,⟹\\,x=-166.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-166$, $y=-91$, and $z=-22.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342022 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3624bdf6-d1bf-5873-8a2a-1542d72f9250" :question/math-academy-id "q-73584" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z &=3 \\\\ y+5z &=-8 \\\\ z &=4,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=4$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+5(4) &=-8\\,⟹\\,y=-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-28$ and $z=4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(-28)-2(4) &=3\\,⟹\\,x=39.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=39$, $y=-28$, and $z=4.$ Or, in vector form $⟨39,-28,4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342023 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a6417a9-7e20-54a9-9651-61368745cde5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73588" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 16 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 16 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 16 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 11\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z &=-2 \\\\ y+2z &=-1 \\\\ z &=11,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=11$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2(11) &=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-23$ and $z=11$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-23)+3(11) &=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-58$, $y=-23$, and $z=11.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨-58,-23,11⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342025 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ecd0e17-9926-5c80-9527-2ab263ac9de2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73592" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-6y+3z &=-6 \\\\ 2x-4y+3z &=-1 \\\\ 2y-4z &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & -6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & -6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the $2$nd row by $\\frac{1}{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342026 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "975451bc-691b-585b-833f-78b85768cebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-73596" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+2y &=1 \\\\ -3x+ky &=-2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true: \n\nIf the system's coefficient matrix is invertible then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements. \n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible then $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$    \n\nAdditionally, the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the two lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2.$ \n\nFinally, notice that if $k=-1$ then \n\n$$\n\\det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}=\\det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=6\\cdot (-1)-2(-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, if $A$ is invertible,  then $k\\ne -1.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the system's coefficient matrix is invertible, then the lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342029 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16a72be1-2bd1-5d5b-86b7-6ea0a4390bbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-73608" :question/problem "Consider the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\n\\det(A)=\\det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the unique solution of  $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342070 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a2d9a59-591c-58a1-9378-a824f178afc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73735" :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,3,6⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨t,t,2⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-1,2,7⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ t & t & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & t \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-3(t\\cdot 7-(-1)\\cdot 2)+6(t\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot t) \\\\  & =-21t-6+12t+6t \\\\  & =-3t-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ -3t-6 & =0 \\\\ -3t & =6 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342071 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "029d2415-156a-588b-85c8-9df58fb943a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73736" :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨t,1,t⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,-1,-1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨3,0,1⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & 1 & t \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1))-(2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1))+t(2\\cdot 0-3\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-t-5+3t \\\\  & =2t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ 2t-5 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =5 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342072 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f00ce7fb-f804-52bb-ae27-0431b21c7460" :question/math-academy-id "q-73738" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "The system corresponding to $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=-2 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx-3y=-2.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-2+3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2+3y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=⟨-2+3y,\\,y⟩,\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342073 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9dd0d4fd-091a-5d63-898d-8efe81821dbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-73742" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342074 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7492471-0301-5306-8f40-5431247eb08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73743" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342075 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1acfcbc-5797-5d52-8f43-51fb0ff9335f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73744" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(1,1,0)$, $B(-2,1,0)$, $C(3,2,0)$, and $D(1,2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)-0+0 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342076 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d447f611-67a9-5187-a844-7f81dc30a0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-73745" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(1,-1,2)$, $B(-2,2,3)$, $C(1,1,1)$, and $D(-1,3,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(2\\cdot (-4)-4\\cdot (-1))-3(0\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-1))+(0\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =12+6+4 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|22|=22.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342077 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eae017c3-e923-584f-9dc9-ad18f462f150" :question/math-academy-id "q-73746" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(0,1,2)$, $B(-1,0,1)$, $C(1,1,0)$, and $D(3,-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2))+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-2))-(1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =4+7+2 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|13|=13.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342078 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b26b4535-e272-5e94-b038-84726ee96584" :question/math-academy-id "q-73748" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(2,-1,0)$, $B(-1,2,-1)$, $C(1,1,2)$, and $D(3,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 2)-3((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)-((-1)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-6+9+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|\\,5\\,|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342079 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0b4636b-3357-5697-b846-0e6a1da3fd28" :question/math-academy-id "q-73751" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-10y &=-5 \\\\ 2x-4y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=4$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=4,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342080 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "824eec75-5ee7-5066-9e8b-de6f124de7ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73752" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-6y &=10 \\\\ -x+3y &=-5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -6 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -6 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=5 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx-3y=5.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=5+3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=5+3y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342081 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed1f88a5-7f29-5728-a62d-81ac2d1ace51" :question/math-academy-id "q-73753" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(-2,1,1)$, $B(2,1,0)$, $C(1,-1,2)$, and $D(3,-2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot (-3))-0-(3\\cdot (-3)-5\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =36-1 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|35|=35.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342103 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f224ea6c-a3b0-5c69-a134-e6c7eff7fb67" :question/math-academy-id "q-73912" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+3y-5z &=6 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n-x+3y-5z=6\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=-6+3y-5z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-6+3y-5z \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$, $z$ (free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6+3y-5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342104 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "061b9ba7-cf18-55a3-aa89-28e4ebe56a2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73913" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 7 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+7z &=-9 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx-2y+7z=-9\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=-9+2y-7z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-9+2y-7z \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$, $z$ (free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9+2y-7z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342105 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b928310c-eb01-592d-b214-59e2de82f6b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73915" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8 & 24\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8 & 24\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8 & 24\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=3 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx+2y-z=3\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=3-2y+z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3-2y+z \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y,z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3-2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342106 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d037d1d-0cc4-5132-92cf-ed832f1634b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73917" :question/problem "Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3} &=6 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-8{x}_{3} &=-8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for the system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342107 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5afdcd66-6368-5354-bd74-a259df30653f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73918" :question/problem "Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+20{x}_{2}-10{x}_{3} &=40 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for the system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 20 & -10 & 40 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 1 & -4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 20 & -10 & 40 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 20 & -10 & 40 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342108 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56215786-6dd7-5900-942c-c24344ff27f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-73927" :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, then $3\\mathbf{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{v}$ by $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{v} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342109 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4f125d7-bde6-5bbc-b86e-a1dc22a6397f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73929" :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{w}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$, then $5\\mathbf{w}=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{w}$ by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\mathbf{w} & =5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5\\cdot 1 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342110 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a045a18-4b26-532b-8e6c-7d683732c9ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-73931" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{x}$, if $3\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 2\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342111 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b51a337-e5d6-556d-a72d-bef903a48688" :question/math-academy-id "q-73933" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{x}$, if $5\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 18 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 3\\mathbf{x} & =5\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{3}(5\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(5\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 18 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -48 \\\\ 51\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342112 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fa0bdb5-f053-5d67-8f95-d145fa39b1e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73939" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ What is $(\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a})\\cdot \\mathbf{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{a}$ by $\\frac{1}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a})\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =2\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+3+1+0+10 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342113 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d756465-9173-5daf-942c-7abdf0168d3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73941" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ What is $(-\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{c})\\cdot \\mathbf{d}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{c}$ by $-\\frac{1}{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{c})\\cdot \\mathbf{d} & =0\\cdot 9+2\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0+8+8-3-6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342114 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e030b11b-7d8c-5bb3-95d3-2268959b30a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73948" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{w}=\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ -3 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf $\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{w}=-2$ and $x>0$, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the equation $\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{w}=-2,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{w} & =-2 \\\\ x\\cdot 3x+2x\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot 2 & =-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6x+4x-10 & =-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2x-8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(3)(-8)}}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{100}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Since we need $x>0$, our final answer is $x=2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342115 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64baab11-7178-5faa-a751-966ea0fbee6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73949" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{l}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ x \\\\ 2 \\\\ x \\\\ 2x\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{m}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf $\\mathbf{l}\\cdot \\mathbf{m}=3$ and $x>3$, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the equation $\\mathbf{l}\\cdot \\mathbf{m}=3,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{l}\\cdot \\mathbf{m} & =3 \\\\ 5\\cdot 1+x\\cdot x+2\\cdot 4+x\\cdot (-1)+2x\\cdot (-3) & =3 \\\\ 5+{x}^{2}+8-x-6x & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+13 & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+10 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Since we need $x>3$, our final answer is $x=5$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342116 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0008ebd1-cf28-5d38-a9cd-5c6dde0f95a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73951" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{c}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{c}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{c}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{c}}_{2}=\\mathbf{d},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$\n." :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{c}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{c}}_{2}=\\mathbf{d},$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-11 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the second and the third  equations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1} & =-16\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}_{1}=-2$ into the third equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-2)+{x}_{2} &=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=3$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =3 \\\\ (-2)+2(3) & =3 \\\\ 4 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a false statement. \n\nSo, the system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342117 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec4fe83-c254-5d51-9484-b88391578898" :question/math-academy-id "q-73952" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the first and the second equation we get\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{2}=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =-7 \\\\ {x}_{1}-(4) & =-7 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-7+4 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & =6 \\\\ 2(-3)+3(4) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ which means\n\n$$\n-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+4{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+4=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342118 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abf235b3-2d59-5bfb-aea0-aa237461f6b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73954" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=16 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$, the second equation by $2$, and adding the resulting equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}_{2}+8{x}_{2} & =48-2 \\\\ 23{x}_{2} & =46 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=2$  into the second equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =-1\\text{ } \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4(2) & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =-9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into our third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & =-18 \\\\ 4(-3)-3(2) & =-18 \\\\ -18 & =-18,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ which means\n\n$$\n-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+2{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-3)+2=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342119 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e8fc794-8de1-566d-a7d1-0246dc2589a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73955" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y-2z &=-5 \\\\ 0x+2y-2z &=-3 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=-5,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342120 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b176b3fe-cf93-51c6-8ce9-e7905742c0c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-73958" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 & 18\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 & 18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -12 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 & 18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -12 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 34\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y-6z &=8 \\\\ 0x-y-12z &=16 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=34\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=34,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342121 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba6ce214-f348-57ed-8c0e-e75dd14f4628" :question/math-academy-id "q-73959" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -12 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -19 & 15\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -12 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -19 & 15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -19 & 15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -9 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y-5z &=6 \\\\ 0x+y+3z &=-4 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=-9,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342122 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2553c2bd-9198-50b3-b6db-dc189cb3e196" :question/math-academy-id "q-73960" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -12 & -6 & 16 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 2 & -20 & -4 & 25\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -12 & -6 & 16 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 2 & -20 & -4 & 25\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -12 & -6 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -20 & -4 & 25\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -12 & -6 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -12 & -6 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -12 & -6 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -11\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-12y-6z &=16 \\\\ 0x+y+2z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=-11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=-11,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342123 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c67076c-502b-504a-869c-d6e3549e0a05" :question/math-academy-id "q-73981" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=9 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the third equation by $-1$ and adding the result to the first equation we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{1} & =7-4 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  we substitute ${x}_{1}=3$ into the third equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 3(3)-{x}_{2} & =4 \\\\ -{x}_{2} & =4-9 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=5$ into the second equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & =9 \\\\ -2(3)+3(5) & =9 \\\\ -6+15 & =9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=5,$ which means\n\n$$\n3{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+5{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+5=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342126 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33debd45-f90c-578d-9c6b-11c54937ac6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73992" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-5 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=-10 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2.5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2.5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-5 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2.5\\,{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2.5(-2)=8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=8-5=3.\n$$\n\nFinally,  substituting ${x}_{2}=3$ and ${x}_{3}=-2$  into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(3)+2(-2)=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=-2,$ which means \n\n$$\n2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+3{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+3-2=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342130 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff5142a4-ab7b-5300-baa5-14f6253ed7fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-74006" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then   we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -12\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-5 \\\\ 0 &=-12.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=-12$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342132 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ca28715-77ca-55f5-9aad-4851b6f70f79" :question/math-academy-id "q-74028" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-9 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting ${x}_{2}=-2,{x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+(-2)+3(-1) & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1}-5 & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=4$, ${x}_{2}=-2$, and ${x}_{3}=-1,$ which means \n\n$$\n4{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=4+(-2)-(-1)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342133 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "237520c7-3369-5c34-87a7-ba4be3c9b705" :question/math-academy-id "q-74032" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ -11{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-11{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-11{x}_{3} &=-18 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -15 & -30\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{15}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this value into  the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(2)=-6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, by substituting ${x}_{2}=-2$ and ${x}_{3}=2$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1}+(-2)+3(2) & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1}+4 & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=1$, ${x}_{2}=-2$, and ${x}_{3}=2,$ which means \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1+(-2)+2=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342134 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "118e9ea0-84b3-5e8f-866f-104da032b6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74035" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 & -4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0.2 & 0.8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0.2 & 0.8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{2}+\\,0.2{x}_{3} &=0.8 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+0.2(-1)=0.8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally,  substituting ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-2$, ${x}_{2}=1$, and ${x}_{3}=-1,$ which means  \n\n$$\n-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-2+1-1=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342138 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8d414ab-92e5-5218-83d9-6fd3ed558d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-74046" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=5.$ Substituting this value in the second and first equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{2}-2(5)=-6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{1}+5=8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=3$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=5,$ which means \n\n$$\n3{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+4{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+5{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+4+5=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342180 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efbc99ee-782d-5cfc-bd9c-e3ca79e0d68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74198" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 0 &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=1.$ \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Col}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342184 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51a492c4-1836-535a-a084-317ef0e2317c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74209" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k-3\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(B).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Col}(B),$ then the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[B\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[B\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & k-3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & k-3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k+2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 0 &=k-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-1,$ which means that $k=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342185 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "035f63e6-ef7b-5c17-ba6e-584735b13fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-74212" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $L=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}k-11 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(L).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Col}(L),$ then the equation $L\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[L\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[L\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 1 & k-11 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -10 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & \\leftrightarrow {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 1 & k-11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 1 & k-11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & k+4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-16\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=10 \\\\ 0 &=k-16.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-16,$ which means that $k=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342186 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a3c8192-bc53-5c3d-a7c3-d44d3533846a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74213" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $M=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2k+10\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(M).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ then the equation $M\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[M\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 2k+10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & \\leftrightarrow {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 2k+10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 2k+10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 15 & 2k+10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2k-10\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 0 &=2k-10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=2k-10,$ which means that $k=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342187 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e93e600-4a01-5ada-842d-9b175b57ee56" :question/math-academy-id "q-74217" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $1-x-y=0$ since $1-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is in $B$, but $2u=⟨2,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since ${2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}≰1.$ So, $B$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $2x+3y-z=0$ since $2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}C=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\2x+3y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5037faf6-6f8a-5ea2-800f-2c136a3bb59e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74218" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $\\mathbf{0}=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under vector addition. Indeed, both $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,1,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,0,1⟩+⟨0,1,1⟩=⟨1,1,2⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xyz=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $\\mathbf{0}=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342189 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ab37f17-4ea0-5e43-8d25-5328ba6ba1aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-74223" :question/problem "The matrix $M$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below.\nDetermine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{m}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{m}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{m}}_{\\mathbf{3}},$ and ${\\mathbf{m}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{m}}_{2}$, and ${\\mathbf{m}}_{\\mathbf{3}}$ are the respective columns of $M.$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -13\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ we need to check if the equation $M\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[M\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -13 & 16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -13 & 16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & \\leftrightarrow {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=10 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2}+2(-1) & =10 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-2 & =10 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} & =12 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+(-4)-5(-1) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+1 & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $M\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{m}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{m}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{m}}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{b}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{m}}_{1}}+(-4)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{m}}_{2}}+(-1)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{m}}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-1)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb81e4a8-c5a7-59da-b168-b278b747b24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74255" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\5x-y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342223 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b39a9eda-2020-5222-b891-a65118115aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74546" :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$, and $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(-2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=8.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+(-2){\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=8\\cdot (-2)=-16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342228 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62066547-f160-5da7-be83-cd71028d6d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-74556" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 6 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & k-6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-9{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-15\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-15.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-15\\,⇒\\,k=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342229 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c201285-f84a-584f-bd1b-b67204ad9977" :question/math-academy-id "q-74557" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k+12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-3.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-3\\,⇒\\,k=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342230 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cef0a842-c20f-55db-9989-525f1aca72a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-74558" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\nk=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342231 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1691e3c5-722a-5803-9898-54e9fb9a633f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74559" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=-8 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 9 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 10 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 10 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 6 & k+6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-18\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-18.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-18\\,⇒\\,k=18.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342237 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e12a2efc-9d88-56c5-96b5-ec530d76269d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74594" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=9 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -32 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -32 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k-18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k-18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-6.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-6\\,⇒\\,k=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342238 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "329f79c0-9230-5c7a-8435-3cce24f63578" :question/math-academy-id "q-74596" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-9 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 10 & -12 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 6 & -8 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -12 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -30 \\\\ 10 & -12 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -30 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k-45\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -30 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+45\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+45.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+45\\,⟹\\,k=-45.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342239 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5c520f5-835e-5cc8-a0fb-53c3d5239f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74597" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k+6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k+6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+4\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+4.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+4\\,⇒\\,k=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342240 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55c44296-8bfb-53c1-b37c-07eab30d99a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-74598" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}$, $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨3,-7,-9⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,-3,-6⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+3t &=2 \\\\ 2s-7t &=-3 \\\\ 3s-9t &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+3t &=2 \\\\ -t &=1 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-t=1\\,⟹\\,t=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+3t & =2 \\\\ -s+3(-1) & =2 \\\\ -s & =5 \\\\ s & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-5,\\,t=-1,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=-5\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-5)\\cdot (-1)=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342241 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6c3f74b-5a27-519e-8d22-1736702759dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-74599" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}$, $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,4,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,-6,-7⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨1,-10,-15⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ 4s-6t &=-10 \\\\ 3s-7t &=-15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -6 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ -2t &=-6 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2t=-6\\,⟹\\,t=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+t & =1 \\\\ -s+3 & =1 \\\\ -s & =-2 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=3,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342243 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcb29558-7233-531f-8a7f-7d0b5b7173b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74602" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}$, $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,7,-6⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,-10,12⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,11,-12⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-2t &=2 \\\\ 7s-10t &=11 \\\\ -6s+12t &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 7 & -10 & 11 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-2t &=2 \\\\ 4t &=-3 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n4t=-3\\,⟹\\,t=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-2t & =2 \\\\ s-2\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =2 \\\\ s+\\frac{3}{2} & =2 \\\\ s & =2-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ s & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=\\frac{1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{3}{4},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}-\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle s\\cdot t=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344944 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c8c4efc9-6266-5ebb-8bcb-7388b1494f8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74605" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},$ $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,3,6⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,-2,4⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨3,19,-10⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 19 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s+t &=3 \\\\ 3s-2t &=19 \\\\ 6s+4t &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 19 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -32\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-32,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344956 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edc80b1b-d469-5071-b24d-3c90bb47fce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74635" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+4y+3z &=0 \\\\ x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means that  we can find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement “The system is consistent dependent” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344957 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0089d19-a4b5-5ec4-beaa-917a294458ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-74637" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=-1 \\\\ x-2y &=1 \\\\ -x-5y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=1 \\\\ 7y &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-\\frac{3}{7}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2\\left(-\\frac{3}{7}\\right)=1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\n \nSo, we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement “The system is consistent independent” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344961 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0c28bd7-70de-59aa-93c9-933cbaca4593" :question/math-academy-id "q-74646" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=2 \\\\ 2x+(k+1)y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-7 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=2 \\\\ (k-7)y &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=7,$ then the second equation is $0=-1,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344968 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4dc6297-c39f-58c6-a8af-023dc6a275e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-74671" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $\\mathbf{x}$ is a vector such that $-\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{w}$ for some $\\mathbf{w}\\in V$, then $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{adding }\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}))+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom  A3}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{adding }-\\mathbf{w}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axioms  A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom  A3}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{adding }\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}))+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom  A3}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{adding }-\\mathbf{w}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom  A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A4}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom  A3}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344969 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdfa86e8-f2dc-5d19-9144-98ae43ab2ede" :question/math-academy-id "q-74688" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-4 \\\\ 3x+2ky &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2k-6 & 10\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-4 \\\\ (2k-6)y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=3,$ then the second equation is $0=10,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344971 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db41cb50-724f-5af7-85df-697ff3307e10" :question/math-academy-id "q-74695" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=-8 \\\\ x+ky &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & k & -2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & k & -2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & k & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & k & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2-4k & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+ky &=2 \\\\ (2-4k)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{1}{2},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=2.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344987 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b184b945-0cbf-54e5-bc87-5c6a9c59df68" :question/math-academy-id "q-74867" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $k$ such that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{b} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4k-8 \\\\ 2k-4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4k-8 &=0 \\\\ 2k-4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344988 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "585342a5-4f43-526c-91d5-902d1e3aac63" :question/math-academy-id "q-74870" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have two free variables, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}$  and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3},$ so the set of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344989 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ac77401-4e26-5c21-810e-811c018a9f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74872" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 6 & -18 & 18 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 6 & -18 & 18 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -18 & 18 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)+3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the set of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}\\right\\}.\n$$"}]}
